.TH FAIL_LOC 4 2026-10-09 "Lustre" "Lustre Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
fail_loc, fail_val, fail_err \- inject failures for debugging and testing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY
.B lctl set_param fail_loc=
.RI [ FLAGS | ] LOCATION
.SY
.B lctl set_param fail_val=
.I VALUE
.SY
.B lctl set_param fail_err=
.I ERROR
.SY
.B lctl get_param fail_loc fail_val
.YS
.SS PROPERTIES
.TP
.B Access Permissions
.br
.BR 644 " | " -rw-r--r--
.TP
.B Scope
.br
Global
.TP
.B Config
.br
Always present
.TP
.B Default
.br
.RB fail_loc= 0
.br
.RB fail_val= 0
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B fail_loc
and
.B fail_val
parameters inject failures, delays and races into the Lustre and LNet
code on the node where they are set, so that tests can exercise error
handling and recovery paths that are otherwise hard to reach.
An injected failure can return errors to applications, evict clients,
crash the node or corrupt the filesystem, so
.B fail_loc
must never be set on a production system.
.PP
The low 16 bits of
.B fail_loc
select one failure location, compiled into the code as an
.B OBD_FAIL_*
or
.B CFS_FAIL_*
constant.
Locations 0x0100 to 0x3fff are in Lustre, 0xe000 to 0xefff in LNet, and
0xf000 to 0xffff in the LNDs.
The list of locations and their meaning is in
.I lustre/include/obd_support.h
and the other headers of the Lustre source, and each test script
names the location it uses in a
.B #define
comment next to the
.B lctl set_param
command that matches the constant being used.
Setting
.B fail_loc
to 0 disables failure injection.
.PP
The high bits of
.B fail_loc
are flags that control when the location fails:
.TP
.BR 0x80000000 " (ONCE)"
Fail only the first time the location is reached.
.TP
.BR 0x20000000 " (SKIP)"
Skip the first
.B fail_val
times the location is reached, then fail.
.TP
.BR 0x10000000 " (SOME)"
Fail only the first
.B fail_val
times the location is reached.
.TP
.BR 0x08000000 " (RAND)"
Fail on average once every
.B fail_val
times the location is reached, where
.B fail_val
is at least 2.
.TP
.BR 0x02000000 " (FAULT)"
Also fail at every location that is checked with
.BR CFS_FAULT_CHECK() .
.PP
At most one of the SKIP, SOME and RAND flags can be set, and ONCE can be
combined with SKIP or RAND.
The kernel sets the FAILED bit 0x40000000 in
.B fail_loc
when the location fails, so
.B lctl get_param fail_loc
shows whether it was reached.
.PP
The
.B fail_val
parameter is the argument of the failure location.
Its meaning depends on the location, for example a delay in seconds,
a target index, or a value to substitute, and it is the count used by
the SKIP, SOME and RAND flags.
The
.B fail_err
parameter is the error returned at some specific
.B fail_loc
locations.
Its meaning depends on the location.
.SH EXAMPLES
Drop the reply to the next metadata update on the MDS
.RB ( OBD_FAIL_MDS_REINT_NET_REP = 0x119 ),
so that the client has to resend it:
.EX
.RB mds# " lctl set_param fail_loc=0x80000119"
.EE
.PP
Delay the next read or write RPC from the client by 10 seconds
.RB ( OBD_FAIL_OSC_DELAY_IO = 0x414 ):
.EX
.RB client# " lctl set_param fail_loc=0x80000414 fail_val=10"
.EE
.PP
Drop about one in ten RPCs sent by the client, so that they time out
and are resent
.RB ( OBD_FAIL_PTLRPC_DROP_RPC = 0x505 ):
.EX
.RB client# " lctl set_param fail_loc=0x08000505 fail_val=10"
.EE
.PP
Stop injecting failures:
.EX
.RB client# " lctl set_param fail_loc=0 fail_val=0"
.EE
.SH AVAILABILITY
The
.B fail_loc
parameter is part of the
.BR lustre (7)
filesystem package since release 1.0.0,
.\" Added in commit 0.0.0-332-gaba91aec60
and the
.B fail_val
parameter since release 1.6.2.
.\" Added in commit 1.6.0.1-181-gceca3d533a
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lctl-get_param (8),
.BR lctl-set_param (8)
